## Per-scenario notes

### Joiner — HRIS trigger, auto path
Mean of 18 seconds, well within the 15-minute SLA target. The range of 9–41 seconds reflects variable API response time in the Okta developer sandbox: sandbox tenants share infrastructure and have higher latency variance than production tenants. In a dedicated production tenant, the variance is expected to be significantly lower. This scenario covers the full path from webhook receipt through SH-4 validation, J-2 duplicate check, J-3 user creation, J-4 baseline group assignment, J-5 package resolution, and J-8 evidence recording.

### Joiner — with approval (manager only)
Mean is not calculated for this scenario. The elapsed time from event receipt to account activation includes the human approver's response time, which is not a property of the automation. The 4-minute minimum reflects a near-immediate approval response in the test environment. The 62-minute maximum reflects an intentionally delayed approval used to test the SH-1b escalation timer. The SLA for this scenario is expressed as an approver target (governed by the escalation policy in `ilm_system_class`) rather than a workflow latency target.

### Joiner — future date (pre-provisioning)
All 10 executions activated the staged account within the ±5-minute window of the configured `startDate`. The scheduled trigger is registered by UTL-2 at the time the joiner flow receives the webhook. Timing accuracy depends on Okta Workflows' scheduled flow execution, which fired within 2–3 minutes of the target time in all test runs.

### Mover — HRIS trigger, non-empty delta, auto
Mean of 22 seconds for a full department transfer with a non-empty group delta. This scenario includes the SH-5 delta calculation, M-5 removal of old groups, M-6 addition of new groups, M-7 profile attribute update, and M-8 evidence recording. The 38-second maximum reflects a run with a larger group delta (7 additions and 3 removals) where each UTL-4 group API call added cumulative latency.

### Mover — title only, no delta
Mean of 9 seconds. When SH-5 returns empty `toAdd` and `toRemove` lists, stages M-4 through M-7 are skipped entirely. The flow updates only the Okta profile attributes (title, managerId if changed) and closes the transaction. This is the fastest mover scenario by design.

### Leaver — same day, central suspension
Mean of 7 seconds from webhook receipt to account suspension (step L-3 completion). This is the most operationally significant result. In the as-is process, the same outcome took anywhere from the same day to five days depending on IT availability and communication chain length. The automated path eliminates this variability entirely: suspension executes within the flow's runtime regardless of time of day, day of week, or IT team availability. The 4-second minimum and 12-second maximum reflect sandbox API variance only.

### Leaver — same day, full deprovisioning
Mean of 31 seconds for the complete leaver path: suspension (L-3), session revocation (L-4), all group removals (L-5), ITSM task generation for manual systems (L-6), critical systems check (L-7), and account deactivation (L-8). The 54-second maximum reflects a run with a larger group set (11 groups removed) where the cumulative API call count was higher.

### Leaver — future date, scheduled
All 10 executions fired within the ±5-minute window of the configured `endDate`. Scheduling behaviour is identical to the pre-provisioning joiner variant — UTL-2 registers the scheduled trigger at webhook receipt time and the full leaver sequence executes on the target date.

### Error — validation failure
Mean of 5 seconds. This measures the time from receiving an incomplete payload to writing the `Clarification-required` record and emitting the SH-2 notification. No Okta user object is created in this path, so the execution is faster than any happy-path scenario.

### Error — API retry and intervention queue
Mean of 48 seconds. This reflects 3 retry attempts with a 10-second base delay (configured for test speed; production default is longer). The 34-second minimum reflects runs where the first retry succeeded. The 71-second maximum reflects runs where all 3 retries failed and the full intervention queue write plus SH-2 alert was required. In all cases, the security-critical leaver suspension (L-3) had already completed before the ITSM connector failure at L-6, confirming that failure at downstream steps does not roll back security-critical actions.

---

## Rate limiting

No rate-limiting events occurred during any test run. The synthetic test volume — 10 executions per scenario, approximately 90 Workflows executions in total — was well below the Okta API rate limit for the developer sandbox (600 API calls per minute at the tenant level).

At the expected production volume of 50 JML events per month, with each event generating approximately 8–12 API calls, the peak instantaneous API call rate is negligible relative to the rate limit. NFR4.2 is met at the expected operational scale.

---

## Sandbox environment note

All results were produced in a shared Okta developer sandbox. Sandbox tenants share underlying infrastructure with other developer accounts, which introduces latency variance not present in dedicated production tenants. The minimum values in the results table are more representative of production performance than the mean. Production deployments are expected to show lower mean latency and significantly lower variance, particularly for scenarios involving multiple sequential API calls (full deprovisioning, non-empty delta mover).

